          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">USB Charging</h3>

          <p>
            The Shark Cyclone PET charges over USB, so it charges in the car, an RV or an office without a dedicated charger.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Removable Battery Platforms</h3>

          <p>
            The DEWALT runs on DEWALT 20V MAX tool batteries and the Hoover on ONEPWR batteries. A spare battery doubles your cleaning time, and if you already own the platform, the vacuum costs less.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-stick" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Handheld Vacuum vs Stick Vacuum: Do You Need Both?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Many stick vacuums convert to handhelds, which raises the question of whether a separate hand vac is worth having.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where a Dedicated Hand Vac Wins</h3>

          <p>
            A dedicated hand vac is lighter, smaller and quicker to grab. It is the tool for the kitchen counter, the car, the stairs and the sofa. Pulling a stick vacuum off its dock and removing the wand for a few crumbs is enough effort that people skip it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where a Stick Vacuum Wins</h3>

          <p>
            For floors, a stick vacuum covers far more ground with more power and runtime. Our guide to the <a href="/best-cordless-vacuum/">best cordless vacuums</a> compares stick models, and the <a href="/best-cordless-vacuum-for-hardwood-floors/">best cordless vacuums for hardwood floors</a> covers bare-floor homes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Common Setup</h3>

          <p>
            Most homes do well with a stick or robot vacuum for floors and an inexpensive hand vac for everything above the floor. Our <a href="/best-robot-vacuum/">robot vacuum guide</a> covers the hands-off option.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How to Keep a Handheld Vacuum Working
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hand vacuums lose suction mostly from neglect. A few habits keep them strong.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Empty After Every Use</h3>

          <p>
            Suction drops sharply as the cup fills. Empty it every time, using the one-touch or no-touch release if your model has one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Wash the Filter Regularly</h3>

          <p>
            Most models here have washable filters. Rinse as the manual says, and let the filter dry completely before refitting it, because a damp filter grows mildew and clogs faster.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Clear Hair From Brushes</h3>

          <p>
            Even anti-tangle brushes collect some hair and thread. Check them every few uses and clear anything caught.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Store It on Charge</h3>

          <p>
            Keep the vac on its dock or charger so it is ready when you need it. A hand vac with a flat battery is the reason most spills end up being swept with a paper towel.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="small-spaces" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Handheld Vacuums for Apartments, Dorms and Small Homes
          </h2>

          <p>
            In a small space, a good handheld can do more of the cleaning than you might expect, and storage space is often the deciding factor.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When a Handheld Can Be Your Main Vacuum</h3>

          <p>
            A studio apartment or dorm room with hard floors and a rug or two can often be kept clean with a light hand vac and a dust mop. The Shark Cyclone PET's XL cup and the Shark UltraCyclone's pet brush cover rugs, upholstery and corners without a full-size vacuum taking up a closet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Storage and Charging</h3>

          <p>
            Think about where the vac will live. The WandVac's dock sits on a counter or shelf, the Cyclone PET charges from any USB outlet, and the BLACK+DECKER models store in a drawer. If the vac is out of sight and out of charge, it will not get used.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When You Still Need a Floor Vacuum</h3>

          <p>
            Wall-to-wall carpet, a larger home or heavy shedding pets still call for a stick or robot vacuum for the floors. Bending over to hand-vacuum a whole carpeted room is slow and hard on the back, so use the hand vac for everything above the floor and for spot clean-ups.
          </p>
        </div>

